32448 / 3 - 1024 = 76458`.\n- That 76458 is then passed as the third launch parameter, so every CTA is *allocated* 76458 bytes of dynamic smem even though", "```\n\n## 1. What the work-partitioning code does\n\n### The two-part split\n\nMarlin normally uses a **single-tile SK** (single-kernel, split-K) scheme: each\nCTA owns one `(m, n)` output tile and iterates over all `k_tiles` of the K\ndimension.  When the number of output tiles is smaller than the grid, that\nbecomes inefficient, so the code falls back to a **two-tile SK** scheme in\nwhich multiple CTAs cooperate on the same output tile and a cross-CTA reduction\nis required.\n\nThe code therefore partitions the grid into two parts:\n\n* **Part 1 \u2013 DP (data parallel).**  `part1_mn_iters` whole passes over the\n  output-tile grid.  In each pass every CTA handles one MN tile, so no\n  cross-CTA reduction is needed.  This is the cheap, preferred mode.\n* **Part 2 \u2013 two-tile SK.**  The remaining `part2_mn_tiles` output tiles are\n  worked on by the whole grid, with K split across CTAs.  Each output tile is\n  produced by several CTAs, and a", " Do not propose code changes.\n\n## Row 1: Routed W4 Marlin (MoE)\n\nLikely reducible. The 42.31 ms cumulative is dominated by the 25.27 ms union\ncomponent, which means most of the time is spent in kernels that are not\noverlapped with anything else on the critical path. The 20.15 ms solo figure\nis the lower bound for the actual compute, so the gap between union and solo\n(5.12 ms) is the portion that could in principle be hidden by better\nscheduling. The real question is whether the 20.15 ms solo is itself\nbound by memory bandwidth for the expert weights, in which case it is not\nreducible without changing the quantisation or the routing fan-out, or whether\nit is bound by kernel launch overhead or suboptimal tile sizes, in which case\nit is.\n\nTo settle this: run the same profile with `CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1` disabled\nand `nsys`/`ncu` kernel-level traces to get per-kernel wall time and\nachieved bandwidth for the top Marlin kernels. Compare achieved bandwidth\nagainst the HBM peak for the GPU. If the kernels are", "We need to analyze the code and the context. The code is `apply_tiered` in `modular_kernel.py`. It runs disjoint expert tiers behind one prepare/finalize pair. The tiers are a list of tuples: (kernel, w1, w2, expert_map). The code has a special case for two tiers and small number of tokens (<= overlap_max_tokens) where it uses an auxiliary stream to overlap the two tiers. It allocates buffers via `_allocate_tiered_buffers` and then runs `run_tier(1)` on `tier_stream` and `run_tier(0)` on the main stream, then waits and combines.\n\nThe context says: the model runs two Marlin kernels per layer on two CUDA streams, one reading weights from HBM and one from pinned host memory through UVA. Both kernels were given a shared `workspace` tensor to save memory. The crash is not deterministic and sometimes presents as a hang at 100% GPU with the host stuck in `cudaStreamSynchronize`.\n\nWe need to diagnose the most likely root cause, explain the mechanism precisely, and give the fix. Also explain why this would present as a hang in some runs and an illegal access in others.\n\nLet's look at the code", "We need to interpret the provided data and answer the questions. The user wrote a probe that launches two identical kernels on two streams, varying only the dynamic shared memory requested per CTA. The table shows:\n\n```\n  dynSmem  occ/SM soloA_ms soloB_ms   serial union_ms ov_frac   peak\n    76800       3 1768.806  900.821 2669.627 2381.854    0.39      3\n    57344       4 1769.038  899.633 2668.671 2292.612    1.00      4\n    48000       4 1770.202  901.475 2671.677 2289.643    1.00      4\n```\n\nColumns:\n- dynSmem: dynamic shared memory per CTA (bytes? probably bytes, but 76800 bytes = 75 KB, 57344 = 56 KB, 48000 = 46.875 KB)\n- occ/SM: occupancy per SM? Probably", "```\n</context>\n```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>```\n</context>``", "```\n\n## Profiling the ExpertMLP\n\n### Sources of overhead (ranked)\n\n1. **Python-level expert loop (dominant).** 256 iterations of pure Python with\n   per-iteration `mask = (topk_ids == e).any(-1)`, `x[mask]`, and `out[mask] +=`\n   launches a huge number of tiny CUDA kernels (comparisons, reductions,\n   boolean indexing, gather/scatter). At batch 4 you typically have 0\u20131 tokens\n   per expert, so most iterations do no useful GEMM work but still pay the full\n   Python + indexing + launch cost. This is the single biggest reason it is\n   slower than the fused Marlin kernel.\n\n2. **Per-expert dense GEMMs on tiny shapes.** Even when an expert has 1 token,\n   `xe @ self.w13[e].t()` is a `(1, hidden) x (hidden, 2*inter)` GEMM. cuBLAS is\n   inefficient at M=1, and you launch two such GEMMs per expert (w13 and w2).\n   The fused kernel groups tokens across experts into block-sparse GEMMs with\n   `block", "We need to analyze the scenario: a split-K GEMM (matrix multiplication) with a launch grid change. The output moved by up to 0.00195 in bfloat16. Every individual run is exactly reproducible (deterministic per configuration). But the golden SHA-256 over a 256-token greedy continuation now mismatches.\n\nWe need to explain:\n1. Why the grid change moves the result at all.\n2. Why the change is deterministic per configuration but different between configurations.\n3. Whether this constitutes a correctness regression.\n4. How to re-establish a defensible correctness gate robust to this class of change without becoming so loose that it stops catching real bugs.\n\nLet's break down the problem.\n\n**Context**: Split-K GEMM is a parallel reduction over the K dimension. In GEMM C = A * B, where A is MxK, B is KxN, C is MxN. Split-K divides the K dimension into multiple parts, each computed by a separate thread block (or group of blocks), and then the partial results are reduced (summed) to get the final C. The order of summation can affect floating-point results due to non-associativity of floating-point arithmetic. In bfloat16", "  We'll use this to sweep\noccupancy and see how many CTAs from the two kernels can actually share an SM. We need to write a CUDA kernel for GH200 (sm_90a) that measures whether two concurrently launched kernels are genuinely co-resident on the same SM, rather than merely overlapping in wall-clock time.\n\nRequirements:\n- record per-CTA start and stop from `%globaltimer` and the SM id from `%smid`\n- take the dynamic shared memory request as a launch parameter so occupancy can be swept\n- run on two non-blocking streams with no cross-stream event waits\n- post-process to report the peak number of CTAs resident on any single SM across both kernels\n\nWe need to give the full kernel plus the host driver, and explain why time-range overlap of the two kernels is not evidence of co-residency.\n\nWe'll use this to sweep occupancy and see how many CTAs from the two kernels can actually share an SM.\n\nLet's design the kernel. The kernel will be launched with a grid of CTAs. Each CTA will record its start time, SM id, and then do some work (maybe a busy loop) to ensure it stays resident for a while, then record stop time. We need to", " Then give me a single concrete\nchange to the kernel that would let me test whether the C2C path is\nmemory-fabric-bound or compute-bound, and what the result would mean.\n\n---\n\n### What the measurements tell you\n\n**The C2C path is not bottlenecked by HBM bandwidth.**  \nThe HBM kernel sustains 3.1 TB/s and the C2C kernel sustains 410 GB/s. When they\nrun concurrently with genuine co-residency, neither slows down. If the C2C\ntraffic were contending for HBM bandwidth, the HBM kernel would drop. It does\nnot. So the C2C stream is not being served out of HBM.\n\n**The C2C path is not bottlenecked by the HBM\u2194Hopper L2 / HBM memory\ncontroller.**  \nSame argument: the HBM kernel's 3.1 TB/s is unchanged in the presence of the C2C\nkernel. The HBM memory subsystem is not the limiter for the C2C traffic.\n\n**The two kernels are not serialised by the scheduler when they are\nco-resident.**  \nIf the scheduler were time-slicing them, the concurrent run would look like", "```\n```python\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright contributors to the vLLM project\n\"\"\"Shared hot/cold Marlin setup and execution for tiered MoE methods.\"\"\"\n\nfrom typing import Any\n\nimport torch\n\nimport vllm._custom_ops as ops\nimport vllm.envs as envs\n\nfrom vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe.oracle.int_wna16 import (\n    make_wna16_moe_kernel,\n    make_wna16_moe_quant_config,\n)\n\n\ndef setup_tiered_moe_kernels(\n    method: Any,\n    layer: torch.nn.Module,\n    group_size: int,\n    num_bits: int,\n) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Build the independent hot and cold kernels for one routed layer.\"\"\"\n    from vllm.config import get_current_vllm_config\n\n    vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()\n    speculative = vllm_config.speculative_config\n    verify_tokens = (\n        speculative.num_speculative_tokens + 1 if speculative is not None else 1\n    )\n    method.tiered_overlap_max_tokens = (\n        verify_tokens * vllm_config.scheduler_config.max_num_seqs", "We need to answer a question a